INT. STAGECOACH WAY STATION, DINING ROOM - NIGHT

A low adobe room. A long plank table, a kerosene lamp, five tin plates. Rain drums the roof.

AMOS GRISWOLD-TAYLOR (60s, station keeper) ladles stew. At the table sit the evening's passengers: CLEMENTINE ROSSITER (30s, a widow's veil pushed back), ISIDRO VALDEZ (40s, a drummer with a sample case), and WENDELL WREN (20s, a seminarian, his collar a size too big). At the head of the table, apart, sits a woman in a trail-worn duster, NELL HAWTHORNE-BEAR (40s).

She has not touched her stew.

CLEMENTINE
Is it always this wet in the territory?

AMOS
Only when somebody's about to die.

ISIDRO
Mr. Griswold, that's not funny.

AMOS
Wasn't meant to be.

WENDELL
Is there a church within fifty miles?

AMOS
There's a hole. Used to be a church.

CLEMENTINE
Does anyone here carry a firearm?

NELL
I do.

They all look at her. She lifts the lid of her stew bowl, peers inside, and sets it down again.

ISIDRO
For what purpose, ma'am?

NELL
The usual.

AMOS
(too brightly)
More bread, anyone?

NELL
Sit down, Amos.

He does.

NELL (CONT'D)
The man who sold you this stew is not the man whose name is on the door.

AMOS
I don't know what you --

NELL
I buried the real Amos Griswold in March. Wasn't a pretty burying.

Nobody moves. The lamp gutters. Isidro's hand slides slowly toward his sample case.

NELL (CONT'D)
Don't.

ISIDRO
Madam, I --

NELL
I've no quarrel with the three of you. It's only him.

Amos laughs, a dry hoarse sound, and reaches under the table. Nell is faster. A single shot, loud as a door slamming, and the gun in his hand skitters across the floor.

He sits very still, clutching his wrist, and for the first time he looks his real age.

AMOS
Who sent you?

NELL
Nobody. I came on my own.

She stands, holsters the revolver, and turns to the others.

NELL (CONT'D)
Finish your supper. The stage leaves at dawn.
